<section aria-labelledby="b2b-wireframes">
  <h2 id="b2b-wireframes">Lo-fi wireframes: 47 screens for four kinds of staff</h2>
  <p>The picks became 47 lo-fi screens in Figma, grouped by who uses them: the front desk, the rink manager, the scorekeeper at the bench, and a tournament organizer. They're gray boxes on purpose. Color comes later, from the skater app's design system. On November 8, I linked them into a clickable lo-fi prototype.</p>

  <h3>Decisions with research behind them</h3>

  <h4>Check-in: the desk marks you here</h4>
  <p>All three rinks check people in on paper, and all three handle walk-ups at the desk. So the main action is a staff member tapping <strong>Mark here</strong> next to a name, with waiver and payment status on the same row. A QR code on the side lets walk-ins sign up and pay on their own phone.</p>
  <p>Not asked yet: how long check-in can take at a busy start, like 51 people booked and one person at the desk. That's a question for the next round of interviews.</p>
  {% figure "img/rinkside/b2b/wf-a2-roster.png",
    "Lo-fi session roster for Stick and Puck, 7 to 8 PM: 42 checked in, 51 booked, 60 capacity, filters for All, Needs attention and Goalies, and a row per skater with role, checked-in status, a Mark here button, waiver and paid status, and Send link or Take payment where something's missing. A side panel shows a walk-in QR code with 9 spots left.",
    "<strong>The session roster.</strong> Everything the desk needs on one row: here or not, waiver, paid.",
    "light" %}
  {% figure "img/rinkside/b2b/wf-a1-today.png",
    "Lo-fi Today screen for the desk: four sessions in order, each with how many are checked in, booked, and the capacity, with a fill bar.",
    "<strong>Today.</strong> Every session with how full it is, so the desk picks one and starts checking in.",
    "light" %}

  <h4>Enter the schedule once</h4>
  <p>At two of the three rinks, the schedule is typed into more than one system by hand. So the manager builds it once in a calendar, or brings it in from a link or a screenshot, and checks what was found before anything goes public. Bringing it in from a link or a screenshot is my call. One caveat: no rink said the copies disagree, so the pitch is saving staff time, not fixing mistakes.</p>
  {% figure "img/rinkside/b2b/wf-b1-calendar.png",
    "Lo-fi week calendar for a rink: calendars for Sheet A, Sheet B, Leagues, Maintenance and Private events can be turned on or off, and sessions sit on a Monday-to-Sunday grid.",
    "<strong>The week.</strong> One calendar per sheet of ice, plus leagues, maintenance, and private events, each one on or off.",
    "light" %}
  {% figure "img/rinkside/b2b/wf-b4-review-import.png",
    "Lo-fi Review import screen: a list of sessions found in an imported schedule, with three flagged rows that need a check, such as a missing price or capacity, and buttons to cancel the import or accept the sure rows.",
    "<strong>Review an import.</strong> Anything the import isn't sure about gets flagged, and nothing goes public until the manager accepts it.",
    "light" %}

  <h4>Score the game at the bench, once</h4>
  <p>All three rinks type game results in a second time at the end of the night, and two keep paper score sheets. So the scorekeeper scores on a phone at the bench, in one flow: tonight's games, big Goal and Penalty buttons, then tap the player. Submitting the game updates the standings. Not asked yet: what device sits at the bench, and whether there's wifi.</p>
  <ul class="compare" role="list">
    <li>{% figure "img/rinkside/b2b/wf-c1-tonight.png", "Lo-fi phone screen, Tonight's games: four games with their times and sheets, one live and three not started.", "<strong>Tonight's games.</strong>", "phone" %}</li>
    <li>{% figure "img/rinkside/b2b/wf-c2-score.png", "Lo-fi phone screen scoring Wolves vs Jets: each team's score with a Goal and a Penalty button, the last three events with Edit, and a Submit game button. A note says standings update on submit and edits are open for 24 hours.", "<strong>Score the game.</strong>", "phone" %}</li>
    <li>{% figure "img/rinkside/b2b/wf-c3-goal.png", "Lo-fi phone screen for a Wolves goal: a keypad of jersey numbers, optional assists, the period, and the time typed in, with Save goal.", "<strong>Add a goal.</strong> Tap the player's number.", "phone" %}</li>
  </ul>

  <h4>See how full each session runs</h4>
  <p>None of the three rinks can see how full their sessions run. So the earnings screen lists every session with how many came and the share of capacity used, lowest first, and flags the ones under 40%.</p>
  {% figure "img/rinkside/b2b/wf-d1-earnings.png",
    "Lo-fi Earnings screen for a week: earned, refunded, net and next payout, a bar chart of earnings per day, an average fill of 56% with 3 sessions under 40% full, and a table of sessions sorted lowest fill first.",
    "<strong>Earnings and fill.</strong> The sessions that lose money are at the top.",
    "light" %}

  <h3>Calls I made without research yet</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Scorekeepers sign in with a short game ID and their own PIN,</strong> so the rink has a record of who kept score. I haven't asked rinks how staff sign in today.</li>
    <li><strong>Whoever submits a game can edit it for 24 hours.</strong> After that, only a manager can, and a manager can undo changes back to the original submission. The 24 hours is my pick, not a number from research.</li>
  </ul>
